---
import Flow from "../../components/Flow.astro";
import CaseLayout from "../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro";
import { research } from "../../content";

const item = research.find((entry) => entry.id === "agentic-data-transfer-optimizer");
if (!item) throw new Error("Agentic Data Transfer Optimizer content is missing");
const sections = [
  { id: "problem", label: "The problem" },
  { id: "hard", label: "Why it is hard" },
  { id: "role", label: "My role" },
  { id: "approach", label: "The approach" },
  { id: "decisions", label: "Decisions" },
  { id: "evaluation", label: "Evaluation plan" },
  { id: "next", label: "Status" },
];
---

<CaseLayout title="Agentic Data Transfer Optimizer · Nieb Hasan Neom" description={item.tagline} heading={item.title} lede={item.tagline} sections={sections}>
  <dl class="facts" slot="facts">
    <div><dt>Status</dt><dd class:list={["status", `tone-${item.tone}`]}>{item.status}</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Built with</dt><dd>{item.stack.join(", ")}</dd></div>
  </dl>

  <section id="problem">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Bulk data movers usually run with a hand-tuned, static concurrency setting. The right number of parallel streams depends on the network path, competing traffic, and host load, and all of them change during a long transfer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="hard">
    <h2>Why it is hard</h2>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>A decision has to be made every probing interval, from noisy signals.</li>
      <li>Language models reason well about patterns but are unreliable at arithmetic.</li>
      <li>An agent that changes concurrency too far or too often can do more damage than a fixed setting.</li>
      <li>The model should run close to the transfer, on modest hardware, without depending on an outside service.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="role">
    <h2>My role</h2>
    <p>I am building the system: the transfer runtime, the telemetry, the two agents, and the guardrails around them.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="approach">
    <h2>The approach</h2>
    <p>
      A sender and receiver move data over TCP through a multiprocessing worker pool with zero-copy <code>sendfile()</code> transfers. At each probing interval the system observes the transfer, two agents reason about it, and the runtime acts on a bounded recommendation.
    </p>
    <Flow
      label="Optimizer control loop"
      steps={[
        { title: "Observe", text: "Read TCP socket statistics, congestion window, retransmissions, RTT variance, and host load." },
        { title: "Diagnose", text: "The Diagnostic agent turns observations and recent history into a structured assessment." },
        { title: "Decide", text: "The Decision agent recommends a concurrency change or holds the current setting." },
        { title: "Enforce", text: "Hard limits, bounded deltas, graduated ramping, and oscillation damping shape the action." },
      ]}
      loop="Each action is evaluated at the next interval, closing an observe, reason, act, evaluate loop."
    />
  </section>

  <section id="decisions">
    <h2>Decisions</h2>
    <ol class="decisions">
      <li><b>Small local models instead of tool-calling agents.</b> The agents are designed around small models that can run on a CPU, so the optimizer needs no external API. Tool-calling agents were ruled out because they need large models.</li>
      <li><b>Keep the model out of arithmetic.</b> Telemetry reaches the model already computed, so it reasons about values instead of calculating them. If the model's output is invalid, the system keeps the previous decision.</li>
      <li><b>The model recommends; the system enforces.</b> No agent output can push concurrency outside the permitted range, repeated reversals are damped, and large changes are ramped in steps.</li>
      <li><b>Make every decision inspectable.</b> Structured memory and a decision journal record what each agent saw and chose. The model layer is pluggable across local Ollama models, fine-tuned Hugging Face checkpoints, OpenAI, and Google Gemini.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="evaluation">
    <h2>Evaluation plan</h2>
    <p>The evaluation is designed to stress the agent in the situations a static setting handles worst:</p>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>several transfers running at the same time;</li>
      <li>a transfer that joins while another runs at a fixed concurrency;</li>
      <li>congested disk I/O;</li>
      <li>packet loss on the nodes in between.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>The prototype is built and evaluation is under way. Results will appear here once the controlled comparisons are complete.</p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
